{"authored" [{"question" "q-32522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i+j-k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1c3c5666e14ee210e237c0c1918a47251637f00dd4def28595311827c8b7293" "basis" 2540 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/state.json" "file_sha256" "d1c3c5666e14ee210e237c0c1918a47251637f00dd4def28595311827c8b7293" "basis" 2540 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-32522" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070857/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e5fe340aa1f5295cfe7753920c41e9c55e95894989c68b4d30c3